<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tatarstan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Tatarstan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tatarstan is a republic of the Russian Federation on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and lower Kama, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>. Its territory was the core of Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan</a>, conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1552. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatar-assr">Tatar Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic</a> was created in 1920, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and Russians make up most of its population of about four million.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tatarstan.jpg" alt="Tatarstan" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Urmas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tatarstan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tatarstan lies in the centre of the East European Plain, about 800 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the Kama rivers. It covers 68,000 square kilometres and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirov">Kirov</a>, Ulyanovsk, Samara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orenburg">Orenburg</a> oblasts and the republics of Mari El, Udmurtia, Chuvashia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkortostan">Bashkortostan</a>. Its capital and largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>. Oil, natural gas and gypsum are its main natural resources.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known organised state within its present boundaries was Volga Bulgaria (c. 700 to 1238), a mercantile state with trade contacts across Inner Eurasia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and the Baltic. Islam was introduced by missionaries from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> around the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-fadlan">Ibn Fadlan</a>'s journey in 922. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan</a> followed, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> conquered the khanate in 1552. In the 19th century the region became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadidism">Jadidism</a>, an Islamic movement that preached tolerance of other religions.</p><p>During the Civil War of 1918 to 1920 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> nationalists tried to found an independent Idel-Ural State with the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, who had at first supported it, arrested members of its parliament in March 1918. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatar-assr">Tatar Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic</a> was established on 27 May 1920, with boundaries that did not include a majority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-tatars">Volga Tatars</a>. On 30 August 1990 Tatarstan declared its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. A constitution approved by about 62 per cent of voters in 1992 defined it as a sovereign state, but the Russian Constitutional Court declared the referendum and constitution unconstitutional. Since the 2002 text, the constitution defines Tatarstan as part of the Russian Federation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tatarstan is a republic of the Russian Federation. Its two state languages are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> and Russian, and the official script is Cyrillic. The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 4,004,809 inhabitants, among them about 2 million Tatars and 1.5 million Russians, with Chuvash, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udmurts">Udmurts</a> in smaller numbers. Most Tatars are Sunni Muslims, and a minority, the Keräşen Tatars, are Orthodox Christians. Three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> lie in the republic: the Kazan Kremlin, the Bulgarian State Museum-Reserve, and the Assumption Cathedral and Monastery of the town-island of Sviyazhsk.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tatarstan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its territory comprised the Bulgar-populated duchies of Bolğar, Cükätäw, Kazan and Qaşan, and covered present-day <b>Tatarstan</b>, Mari El, Chuvashia, Mordovia and parts of Udmurtia and Bashkortostan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…centuries these groups settled in the forest-steppe around the confluence of the Volga and the Kama, in what is now <b>Tatarstan</b>, and established their rule over the Finnic and other peoples of the region, among them ancestors of the Mari, Udmurts and Mordvins, as well as the Burtas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…as one of the world’s oldest religions, and since the dissolution of the Soviet Union it has spread among intellectuals in Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, <b>Tatarstan</b> and Bashkortostan and is undergoing an organised revival in Siberia; Nursultan Nazarbayev and Askar Akayev called it the “natural” religion of the Turkic peoples.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
